# CI pipelines

Applies on top of `AGENTS.md` sections 6, 9 and 11.

- Each build works in its own workspace; nothing is shared between concurrent builds. Cleanup is
  best effort with a timeout and never fails the build.
- Scripts called by the pipeline fail fast; the pipeline itself keeps logs and reports even when a
  step fails, so the evidence survives the failure.
- Branches the pipeline generates have collision-free names (for example with a timestamp).
- Status messages (commit status, comments, notifications) are published as early as possible and
  idempotently, with a final catch-up step, so a late failure does not erase earlier feedback.
- Notify the person who started the run by default; wider recipient lists only with a reason.
- State explicitly which degraded results are acceptable and which block publishing or merging.
- Risky or destructive steps have a dry-run switch.
- Validate the pipeline definition with the platform's linter before committing, when one exists.
- A green status is read together with how the run was triggered and which checks it skipped.
